{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01C3xUyyLkwhhn3mgnnEGjG8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the research project carefully to define three tiers (best/median/worst) for Dream simulation.\n\nKey facts from the data:\n- Project: symb_invariant_consensus (SICA - Symbolic Invariant Consensus Aggregation)\n- Core finding: Self-extracted FOL constraints don't improve over majority voting (SC)\n- Oracle experiment (exp-021): Gold FOL + MAX-SAT = +8.82pp over SC (p<0.001), proving aggregation mechanism works with good constraints\n- Self-extracted SICA ≈ SC across all conditions\n- Target: EMNLP 2026 Main (ARR May 25)\n\n\n- Looking at the experimental results across different models and datasets: Qwen-14B shows minimal gains (+0.25pp), Mistral-7B shows no improvement, LLaMA-8B variants are mixed with one showing +6.67pp but on a small sample (n=60), DeepSeek-R1-7B actually decreases performance, and StrategyQA results are similarly modest at +2.0pp.\n\n- The newer experiments reveal a clearer pattern: LLaMA-8B on FOLIO drops performance by 2.45pp, but the Oracle version jumps to +8.82pp with statistical significance, suggesting the approach works when constraints are perfect. Temperature variations don't affect the gap, constraint ablations show MaxSAT performs no better than simpler baselines, and K ablation indicates SICA consistently underperforms SC across all values.\n\n- Several experiments are still pending including a formal K ablation study and a GSM8K pilot currently running, plus some baseline comparisons and CPU analyses. The core narrative here is about the \"capability trap\" — this is fundamentally a negative-result paper that uses these findings to explain why the approach fails in practice despite theoretical promise.\n\n- The key claims I'm building toward are that self-extracted constraints lack the discriminative power needed, the empirical operating regime shows SICA and SC performing equivalently everywhere, and C0 serves as a necessary but insufficient diagnostic. What remains uncertain is whether the pending experiments will cleanly support this story, how the per-problem analysis will look, and whether the constraint precision patterns match expectations.\n\nIn the best scenario, all remaining tests confirm the equivalence claim, the per-problem breakdown shows clear non-discrimination, GSM8K exhibits the predicted low C0, and the constraint ablation evidence holds up. The median case involves noisier results with maybe one condition showing marginal gains that complicate the \"empty regime\" framing, requiring me to soften some language. Worst case would be a statistically significant SICA improvement somewhere that breaks the universality claim, or challenges to the mechanism evidence like constraint quality issues, or questions about whether the oracle result generalizes beyond FOLIO.\n\nI'm tracking several key metrics to evaluate how the story holds: the SICA-SC gap across conditions, constraint precision as a measure of discriminative power, the relationship between per-problem vote entropy and constraint exclusion, the C0 rate on GSM8K, and how clearly the per-problem figure tells the narrative. constraint precision ~0.50 across all diversity levels\n- GSM8K shows C0 < 20% (as predicted for strong model on easy math), adding another data point to C0 diagnostic\n- Constraint ablation data from exp-013 is clean and reproducible\n- exp-011 n=60 +6.67pp is clearly contextualized as noise (exp-019/020 at n=200+ show null)\n- Paper claims hold as stated, no adjustment needed\n\n**Median**:\n- GSM8K shows unexpected pattern (e.g., C0 > 20% but still SICA ≈ SC, or C0 < 20% but for different reasons than predicted)\n- F2 hero figure shows the pattern but with enough scatter that the story requires more nuanced telling\n- One or two conditions remain n=60 and can't be powered up in time\n- Need to soften Claim 3 (C0 as diagnostic) because the correlation picture is messy, though this is still publishable at EMNLP with hedged claims\n\n**Worst**:\n- A condition shows significant positive SICA gain (breaking universality of null), or per-problem analysis reveals constraints have discriminative power in certain subdomains despite SICA not helping, or GSM8K shows SICA > SC with meaningful gap\n- Core \"capability trap\" framing needs revision — maybe reframe as \"conditions under which self-extraction fails\" rather than \"always fails\"\n- Still targeting EMNLP but as a more nuanced \"when does it work vs not\" paper\n\nNow I'm thinking through the specific number ranges for the SICA-SC gap across all conditions: best case has everything within [-2, +2]pp with no significance anywhere, median case has most within [-3, +3]pp with possibly one marginal condition at the boundary. p<0.1\n- Worst: one condition Δ > +4pp with p < 0.05, breaking universality\n\nFor constraint precision (per-problem):\n- Best: mean precision 0.48-0.52 (indistinguishable from chance)\n- Median: mean precision 0.50-0.55 (slightly above chance but not meaningful)\n- Worst: mean precision > 0.55 in some subset (constraints have some signal, complicating the \"non-discriminative\" claim)\n\nFor Oracle gap:\n- Already fixed at +8.82pp (p<0.001) — this is done\n- But generalizability varies:\n  - Best: reviewers accept single-domain oracle as sufficient causal evidence\n  - Median: reviewers want oracle on second domain, which we don't have\n  - Worst: reviewers question FOLIO-specific oracle as not generalizable\n\nFor F2 hero figure, I'm looking at whether diversity and constraint quality cleanly separate or if there's messy overlap that complicates the narrative. The C0 diagnostic is interesting too — ideally it cleanly splits reasoning models from instruction models with temperature ablations showing the gap holds. Now I'm diving back into the exp-011-llama8b-v3 results to ground this in actual data, checking the effect size and subset performance.\n\nThe LLaMA-8B results at scale show null effects, though the pilot was noisier. The remaining experiments that could shift the story are the GSM8K pilot currently running and the F2 per-problem analysis. I'm defining success tiers more carefully: best case has all remaining data cleanly confirming the capability trap with publication-quality F2 figures and GSM8K adding a fourth domain.\n\nIn the median scenario, the data mostly holds but with noise and some claims need softening. Worst case is if the mechanism evidence gets challenged or the per-problem analysis doesn't clearly support the non-discriminative narrative, forcing a reframe from universal capability trap to one with identified boundary conditions.\n\nNow I'm thinking through which metrics actually matter for tracking this: the maximum SICA-SC gap across remaining experiments, constraint precision from the per-problem analysis, the F2 interpretability as a correlation measure, and the GSM8K performance metrics.\n\nDefining the YAML structure with tiers... Looking at the parameter ranges and narrative direction, the core findings hold up across all conditions—the capability trap characterization is consistently supported, C0 provides a clean diagnostic signal, and the oracle separation result is the strongest contribution. The analysis is shaping up as a clean negative result with precise mechanistic insights and practical diagnostic value, exactly what this type of EMNLP paper should deliver.\n\nBut there are some nuances in the median case that soften the claims slightly. The GSM8K C0 performance runs higher than expected at 20-35%, and while SICA tracks closely with SC, I can't fully rule out a small positive effect. The F2 scatter plot shows real heterogeneity across problem subdomains—some constraint precision estimates land at 0.53-0.58, which is marginally above random but not enough to overturn the main result. The LLaMA-8B signal in one experiment shows a +6.67 percentage point gain, but with only 60 samples it's underpowered and will need careful framing as suggestive rather than conclusive. The ablation study relies on proxy formulas rather than the original Z3 constraints, which introduces some methodological distance from the core claims.\n\nOn the oracle side, I'm only seeing results from FOLIO, which limits how far I can generalize. The remaining effect sizes cluster in a modest range—somewhere between -0.5 and 1.5 percentage points in the meta-analytic estimate, with constraint precision averaging around 0.50-0.56. The capability trap framing still holds up as the organizing principle, but I need to be more precise about where the evidence is strongest and where it's more suggestive.\n\nI'm adjusting the three main claims to be more measured: constraints aren't entirely lacking discriminative power, just insufficient in most conditions; the operating regime isn't completely empty, but we haven't found statistically significant gains in the tested setup; and while C0 does seem necessary, the insufficiency argument needs more careful hedging. A powered replication on LLaMA-8B for one condition might shore this up, but realistically the paper is shifting from claiming a definitive mechanism characterization to presenting a systematic empirical study with provisional findings. It's still competitive for EMNLP, though the contribution leans more toward benchmark and diagnostic value rather than deep mechanistic insight.\n\nThe worst-case scenario would be if the core null hypothesis breaks down—if we find conditions where the constraint actually does help significantly, or if the per-problem analysis reveals that constraint precision has a bimodal distribution where some problems show strong precision but get diluted by low-precision ones, or if the MaxSAT ablation outperforms the simpler baseline on certain subsets. Any of these would require fundamental reframing of the narrative, though the data would still support an EMNLP submission.\n\nThe key concern is that the +10pp signal on FOLIO in exp-011 might not be explainable as pure noise, and reviewers could push back on the generality of claims given that the oracle was only tested on FOLIO and the self-extracted constraints only on Qwen-14B. I'm tracking the plausible ranges for remaining effect sizes, constraint precision distributions, and GSM8K performance gaps to see where the evidence might crack, with the reframing direction shifting from \"universal capability trap\" to \"condition-dependent phenomenon.\"\n\nNow I'm reconceptualizing the paper's narrative entirely—instead of presenting this as a negative result, I'm reframing it as a boundary-condition analysis that identifies when self-extracted constraints actually help versus hurt. The new title and claims structure would pivot to showing that capability trap isn't universal but depends on specific conditions like model capacity, domain formalizability, and constraint precision thresholds. The oracle experiments still validate the aggregation mechanism itself, and I'm proposing C0 plus constraint precision as a joint deployment decision framework rather than relying on C0 alone. This shifts from three negative claims to one positive boundary condition, one mechanistic explanation of failure modes, and one diagnostic framework.\n\nThe risk is that this more nuanced story might feel less clean to reviewers, potentially reading as incremental rather than a clear contribution, but it could also resonate as more sophisticated and interesting. I'm double-checking the metric consistency now—for this negative-result framing, lower deltas confirm the null hypothesis, so I need to verify that best, median, and worst values align properly across remaining_max_abs_delta_pp, constraint_precision_mean, and the non-discriminative fraction metric.\n\nFor GSM8K, I'm checking that the ranges make sense too: lower percentages and deltas closer to zero support the null, while larger positive gaps would break it. The meta-analytic delta also needs to stay centered near zero for the story to hold.\n\nNow I'm refining the correlation metric between C0 and constraint quality—for the capability trap narrative, I want to show no meaningful relationship, so the best range should be tight around zero while worst allows stronger positive correlation that would contradict the story. The boundaries between categories can overlap slightly without issue.\n\nThis framework looks solid, so I'm ready to finalize the output.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19930,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":19930},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3895088c-8f70-4561-87e7-5185b6e929c1","uuid":"195c5402-8cfc-4fbe-af38-04f3970b9dda"}
